OpenClaw v2026.8.1: commit auth metadata before archiving legacy credentials.

Base: ea806575e6450e4d1efdfc72c19f04be982a1b9b.

The dedicated auth migration owner can move AWS SDK markers into auth.profiles
and remove imported inline config credentials. A host that writes the returned
config only after migration can lose the only recoverable marker source if that
write fails after the owner has archived the legacy JSON.

Add an optional host config-persistence hook after each candidate's verified
SQLite import and config changes, but before creating terminal archive receipts.
Marker-only and config-only candidates use the same hook. A rejected commit is
caught by the existing per-candidate handler while source bytes remain in place;
the host must report the failure and retry from freshly loaded configuration.
Existing Doctor callers that omit the hook retain their current behavior.

LobsterAI supplies its core-only, optimistic-concurrency-checked config writer
inside the existing stopped-Gateway maintenance lock. The owner keeps exclusive
responsibility for credential parsing, SQLite import, verification, and archives.

Regression coverage: tests/openclawAuthProfileMigration.integration.test.ts in
LobsterAI, executed against a runtime rebuilt with this patch. In addition to the
normal migration cases, verify marker-only config persistence and failed config
commit followed by retry before retiring this patch upstream.

